---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../../data/site';
import { FUNERAL_SUPPORT_PAYMENT as FSP } from '../../data/funding';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Our prices', url: '/funeral-costs/' },
  { name: 'Help with funeral costs', url: '/funeral-costs/help-with-funeral-costs/' },
];

const money = (n: number) => `£${n.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: 2 })}`;

const faq = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How much is Funeral Support Payment in Scotland?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: `In ${FSP.taxYear}, Funeral Support Payment covers burial or cremation costs in full, plus a flat rate of ${money(FSP.flatRate.withoutPlan)} towards other funeral expenses. Where the person had a fully paid funeral plan, the flat rate is ${money(FSP.flatRate.withPaidPlan)}.`,
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can Funeral Support Payment be paid to the funeral director?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. It can be paid directly to the funeral director, so the money never has to pass through the family. Only one person can receive it for any one funeral.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How long do I have to apply for Funeral Support Payment?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'You can apply from the date of death up to six months after the funeral takes place. You do not have to wait until the funeral has happened.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Page
  title="Help With Funeral Costs in Scotland"
  heading="Help with funeral costs"
  description={`Funeral Support Payment in Scotland ${FSP.taxYear}: who can claim, how much you get, and how to apply. We can help you apply, and it can be paid straight to us.`}
  standfirst="If money is a worry, say so at the start. There is usually more help available than people expect, and we will help you get it."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schema={faq}
>
  <p>
    Nobody should be arranging a funeral while frightened of the bill. Please tell us
    early if cost is a concern — it is an ordinary thing to say, we hear it every week,
    and it changes what we suggest rather than what we think of you.
  </p>

  <h2>Funeral Support Payment</h2>
  <p>
    This is the main help available in Scotland. It is paid by Social Security Scotland
    and, unlike a loan, it does not have to be repaid.
  </p>

  <h3>Who can claim</h3>
  <p>
    You may be able to claim if you live in Scotland, you are responsible for the funeral,
    and you or your partner receive one of the qualifying benefits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    {FSP.qualifyingBenefits.map((benefit) => <li>{benefit}</li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>
    There are rules about your relationship to the person who died, and about whether
    another relative might be expected to pay instead. They are not always obvious.
    Ask us — we deal with this constantly and can usually tell you straight away whether
    it is worth applying.
  </p>

  <h3>How much you get</h3>
  <p>The payment has more than one part.</p>
  <ul>
    {
      FSP.covers.map((part) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{part.title}.</strong> {part.detail}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <Callout title="Things worth knowing">
    <ul>
      {FSP.keyFacts.map((fact) => <li>{fact}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h3>How to apply</h3>
  <p>
    Online at{' '}
    <a href={FSP.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mygov.scot</a>, by telephone to Social Security
    Scotland on 0800 182 2222, or on paper. You will need the death certificate details
    and our invoice or estimate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The application has to be made by you or your partner</strong> — as your
    funeral director, we're not able to apply on your behalf. But bring what you have to
    our funeral home and we will go through the form with you, and we can give Social
    Security Scotland our details so the money comes straight to us once you've applied,
    rather than sitting in your account.
  </p>
  <p>{FSP.covidBackdatingNote}</p>

  <h2>If the person had a funeral plan</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us as early as you can, whoever the provider is. A plan changes what is already
    paid for and reduces what you can claim, so it matters. If you are not sure whether
    there was one, we can help you find out.
  </p>

  <h2>Children's funerals</h2>
  <p>
    There is no burial or cremation fee for anyone under 18 in Scotland, including a
    stillborn baby, and we make no charge of our own for a child's funeral.{' '}
    <a href="/funeral-services/childrens-funerals/">More about children's funerals</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Other help</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The person's estate.</strong> Funeral costs are usually the first thing paid
      from an estate, and banks will often release money for a funeral bill before probate
      is settled. We can invoice the estate directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Employer and pension schemes.</strong> Many pay a death-in-service or
      funeral benefit. Worth asking, and easily forgotten.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trade unions and armed forces charities.</strong> Both frequently help with
      funeral costs for members and veterans.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A simpler funeral.</strong> A{' '}
      <a href="/funeral-services/direct-cremation/">direct cremation</a> costs
      considerably less, and a gathering can be held later, in your own time, for very
      little.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If none of that is enough</h2>
  <p>
    Telephone us anyway. We would far rather have the conversation than have a family go
    without a funeral they can bear, or take on a debt they cannot manage. There is
    almost always something that can be worked out.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="accent">
    <p>
      <a href={CONTACT.phoneHref} class="font-semibold">{CONTACT.phone}</a> — any hour, no
      charge for asking.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
    Figures shown are for the {FSP.taxYear} tax year and were last checked on{' '}
    {new Date(FSP.lastVerified).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
      day: 'numeric',
      month: 'long',
      year: 'numeric',
    })}. Rates are reviewed by the Scottish Government each April. For the current
    official position, see <a href={FSP.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">mygov.scot</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
